# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 3 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 has "noncontra" and "noncontra → ¬demo"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has "noncontra → ¬demo"; they differ when only "there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" is true, which the second allows; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2, to: Suppose "there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" is true and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" is false. Is S2, "That there are no demonstrative arguments in the case seems evident; since it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change, and that an object, seemingly like those which we have experienced, may be attended with different or contrary effects.", true then?
hume-induction.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.2 have "conceivable → noncontra ∧ ¬demo"; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has "conceivable → noncontra" and "noncontra → ¬demo"; they differ when only "there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "there is a demonstrative argument (concerning relations of ideas) that the future will be conformable to the past" and "it implies no contradiction that the course of nature may change" are true and "a change in the course of nature (e.g. salty snow, trees flourishing in December) is intelligible and can be distinctly conceived" is false. Is S5, "Now whatever is intelligible, and can be distinctly conceived, implies no contradiction, and can never be proved false by any demonstrative argument or abstract reasoning à priori.", true then?
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: S9 is read 3 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 has "founded_cause ∧ from_exp ∧ presuppose → (moral → circular)"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 has "founded_cause ∧ from_exp ∧ presuppose → circular"; they differ when only "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the first allows; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has nothing from it
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: S9: answered no, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true and "there is a sound probable (moral) argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that the future will be conformable to the past" and "proving that the future will be conformable to the past by probable arguments goes in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question" are false. Is S9, "To endeavour, therefore, the proof of this last supposition by probable arguments, or arguments regarding existence, must be evidently going in a circle, and taking that for granted, which is the very point in question.", true then?
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.2 have "circular → ¬moral"; hume-induction.logic.r2.3 has "founded_cause ∧ from_exp ∧ presuppose → circular" and "circular → ¬moral"; they differ when only "there is a sound probable (moral) argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that the future will be conformable to the past", "all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect", "our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience" and "all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past" are true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-induction.logic.r2 has "¬moral"; hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 have "¬arg_exists"; they differ when only "there are arguments that engage us to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of future judgement" is true, which the first allows
hume-induction.md:3: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2, to: Suppose "there are arguments that engage us to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of future judgement" is true and "there is a sound probable (moral) argument, regarding matter of fact and existence, that the future will be conformable to the past" is false. Is the conclusion, "But that there is no argument of this kind, must appear, if our explication of that species of reasoning be admitted as solid and satisfactory.", true then?
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if (all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect and our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience and all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past), then proving that the future will be conformable to the past by probable arguments goes in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question"; hume-induction.logic.r2 does not
hume-induction.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps hume-induction.logic.r2.2 and hume-induction.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect and our knowledge of the relation of cause and effect is derived entirely from experience and all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past), then proving that the future will be conformable to the past by probable arguments goes in a circle, taking for granted the very point in question"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
